WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.840
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.660
Today, we will see how a small change to an
already existing learning-based technique

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:14.570
can result in a huge difference in its results.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:19.019
This is StyleGAN2, is a technique that appeared
in December of 2019.

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:26.079
It is a neural network-based learning algorithm
that is capable of synthesizing these eye-poppingly

00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:31.470
detailed images of human beings that don’t
even exist.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.500
This is all synthetic.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:39.460
It also supports a cool feature where we can
give it a photo, then, it embeds this image

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:46.570
into a latent space, and in this space, we
can easily apply modifications to it.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.980
Okay…but what is this latent space thing?

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.630
A latent space is a made-up place where we
are trying to organize data in a way that

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:00.410
similar things are close to each other.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:05.509
In our earlier work, we were looking to generate
hundreds of variants of a material model to

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:08.899
populate this scene.

00:01:08.899 --> 00:01:13.970
In this latent space, we can concoct all of
these really cool digital material models.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:18.280
A link to this work is available in the video
description.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:25.289
StyleGAN uses walks in a similar latent space
to create these human faces and animate them.

00:01:25.289 --> 00:01:28.399
So, let’s see that.

00:01:28.399 --> 00:01:34.139
When we take a walk in the internal latent
space of this technique, we can generate animations.

00:01:34.139 --> 00:01:37.299
Let’s see how StyleGAN2 does this.

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:42.790
It is a true miracle that a computer can create
images like this.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.719
However, wait a second.

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:49.450
Look closely…Did you notice it?

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.420
Something is not right here.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:57.289
Don’t despair if not, it is hard to pin
down what the exact problem is, but it is

00:01:57.289 --> 00:02:01.420
easy to see that there is some sort of flickering
going on.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.469
So, what is the issue?

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:09.930
Well, the issue is that there are landmarks,
for instance, the beard, which don’t really,

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:16.330
or just barely move, and essentially, the
face is being generated under it with these

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:17.730
constraints.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:22.100
The authors refer to this problem as texture
sticking.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.680
The AI suffers from a sticky beard if you
will.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.500
Imagine saying that 20 years ago to someone,
you would end up in a madhouse.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:38.490
Now, this new paper from scientists at NVIDIA
promises a tiny but important architectural

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:39.640
change.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.560
And we will see if this issue, which seems
like quite a limitation, can be solved with

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.790
it, or not.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:51.920
And now, hold on to your papers, and let’s
see the new method.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.390
Holy Mother of Papers.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.860
Do you see what I see here?

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:03.720
The sticky beards are a thing of the past,
and facial landmarks are allowed to fly about

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.350
freely.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:10.730
And not only that, but the results are much
smoother and more consistent, to the point

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:15.660
that it can not only generate photorealistic
images of virtual humans.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.470
Come on, that is so 2020.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:22.030
This generates photorealistic videos of virtual
humans!

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:28.880
So, I wonder, did the new technique also inherit
the generality of StyleGAN2?

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.840
Let’s see.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:39.900
We know that it works on real humans, and
now, paintings and art pieces, yes, excellent,

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.980
and of course, cats, and other animals as
well.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.600
The small change that creates these beautiful
results is what we call an equivariant filter

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:56.160
design, essentially this ensures that finer
details move together in the inner thinking

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.160
of the neural network.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.600
This is an excellent lesson on how a small
and carefully designed architectural change

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.240
can have a huge effect on the results.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:12.050
If we look under the hood, we see that the
inner representation of the new method is

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:15.400
completely different from its predecessor.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:22.760
You see, the features are indeed allowed to
fly about, and the new method even seems to

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:28.820
have invented a coordinate system of sorts
to be able to move these things around.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.870
What an incredible idea.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:35.940
These learning algorithms are getting better
and better with every published paper.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.830
Now, good news!

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:44.530
It is only marginally more expensive to train
and run than StyleGAN2, and less good news

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:50.200
is that training these huge neural networks
still requires a great deal of computation.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:56.400
The silver lining is that if it has been trained
once, it can be run inexpensively for as long

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.730
as we wish.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:04.050
So, images of virtual humans might soon become
a thing of the past, because from now on,

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.280
we can generate photorealistic videos of them.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:06:03.280
Absolutely amazing.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.069
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

